IIUC, different PersistentVolumeSources have inconsistent validation on secret references, e.g. some doesn't require name / namespace. Today there can be data stored in etcd, where the name / namespace is missing. 

It is unclear if we should tighten the validation though. For example, if a PersistentVolumeSource implementation treats a secret reference with empty name / namespace as nil and works, it might not worth stopping people from writing such data. It would be good to tighten validation only in places where such data produces a fatal error 100% of the time. 

Therefore, it would be easier to review if we can separate tightening validation on a source-by-source basis. Would you mind:
1.  Refactor the existing functions into a common function, but keep the existing behavior. For example, 
```
func ValidateSecretReference(secretRef *core.SecretReference, fldPath *field.Path, requireName, requireNS, validateNameFormat, validateNSFormat) field.ErrorList {
  if requireName {
    // secretRef.Name cannot be empty
  }
  if requireNS {
    // secretRef.Namespace cannot be empty
  }
  if validateNameFormat {
    // secretRef.Name is DNSSubdomain
  }
  if validateNSFormat {
    // secretRef.Namespace is DNSLabel
  }
}
```
Here `validateISCSIPersistentVolumeSource` should invoke the function with the following booleans to keep the existing behavior. 
```
ValidateSecretReference(iscsi.SecretRef, fldPath.Child("secretRef"), true, false, false, false)
``` 
2. For each source, open a new PR to tighten the validation only if otherwise the data produces a fatal error 100% of the time. We want to make sure we are backwards-compatible.
